xnu 0351: the console stops painting the panel when the UI takes it

Measured 2026-11-17 on a boot whose UI was live: the screen showed kernel log
text over the App Library, and the ring counted 3.4 million characters painted
on the panel in one session -- about six lines a second. The bootstrap console
paints the log tail by default even after the compositor owns the panel, and a
scroll moves the frame buffer's own pixels up half a screen and fills the rows it
frees with black; with the compositor only repainting what changed, that is a
blurred backdrop that comes out sheared and blocky and turns black after a few
seconds.

Three changes, all of them about who owns the panel:

- The framebuffer quiets the text console at its first swap unless
  mondrianfb_noconsole=0 asks for it. The ring is unaffected (it is fed before
  any quiet flag) and a panic still paints.
- A panic that painted before the UI existed leaves early_panicking set, and
  from then on every log line and status row paints over the UI for the rest of
  the boot: mondrian_early_ui_owns() forgets that state at the first swap, and a
  new panic sets it again.
- A kernel fault under copyin/copyout is recovered and happens on any boot;
  it painted six register rows at the top of the panel each time. Only the
  fault that is about to panic paints now, and it turns the console on for
  itself (mondrian_early_panic_begin()).

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/mondrian_early.c b/osfmk/arm64/mondrian_early.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/mondrian_early.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/mondrian_early.c
@@ -311,7 +311,7 @@
 	 * UI has taken the panel sits on top of it until the next frame -- or
 	 * forever, in the two rows the console keeps below its text area.
 	 */
-	if (!early_text && !early_panicking) {
+	if (!panel_text_allowed() || (!early_text && !early_panicking)) {
 		return;
 	}
 	/* By position: the console cursor belongs to the log thread. */
@@ -321,7 +321,7 @@
 void
 mondrian_early_status(const char *s)
 {
-	if (!early_text && !early_panicking) {
+	if (!panel_text_allowed() || (!early_text && !early_panicking)) {
 		return;
 	}
 	hud_row_text(EARLY_HUD_ROW0, s);
@@ -338,6 +338,56 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * The UI has taken the panel (MondrianFramebuffer.cpp, its first swap): the
+ * console keeps the ring but paints no more pixels. A panic that painted
+ * before that point and left early_panicking set -- one the machine survived --
+ * would otherwise put every later log line and every status row over the UI for
+ * the rest of the boot, and a console scroll moves the frame buffer's own
+ * pixels up half a screen and blacks the rows it frees. A new panic sets the
+ * flags again.
+ */
+void
+mondrian_early_ui_owns(void)
+{
+	early_panicking = 0;
+	early_forced = 0;
+	early_text = 0;
+	early_started = 0;
+	early_row = 0;
+	early_col = 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * A kernel fault that is about to panic: its registers are worth the screen,
+ * and at that point nothing has painted it yet (a recovered fault does not come
+ * here, see sleh.c), so the console is turned on for it.
+ */
+void
+mondrian_early_panic_begin(void)
+{
+	early_panicking = 1;
+	early_forced = 1;
+	early_row = 0;
+	early_col = 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The status rows follow the console off the screen: with the panel taken from
+ * the console (mondrianfb_noconsole=1, the board's default) the scheduler's
+ * "st" row and the framebuffer's "SC"/console rate used to keep painting over a
+ * live UI whenever a panic had set early_panicking, and one of them is a filled
+ * black band across the panel. A panic still paints: its own rows go through
+ * hud_row_text(), not through these.
+ */
+static bool
+panel_text_allowed(void)
+{
+	extern int mondrian_fbcon_quiet;
+
+	return mondrian_fbcon_quiet == 0;
+}
+
+/*
  * A panic row, written by position instead of through the console cursor.
  * The log thread paints the console from another CPU and owns early_row and
  * early_col; a panic that shared them lost its first rows to the painter's
@@ -523,7 +573,7 @@
 	 * the HUD row painting "st <mpidr> s <stage>" over the UI for the whole
 	 * boot, the line the user saw advancing.
 	 */
-	if (!early_text && !early_panicking) {
+	if (!panel_text_allowed() || (!early_text && !early_panicking)) {
 		return;
 	}
 	early_forced = 1;
